{
  "image_name": "4e554e071765fa44660d071db5cbefe7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how metabolic enzymes and stress signals influence tumor-cell survival, senescence, and apoptosis. It presents several parallel pathways centered on glycolytic proteins and mitochondria.\n\nAt the top, FBP1 appears to promote phosphorylation of TERT at S227, which prevents TERT from entering the nucleus; nuclear TERT is linked to increased senescence. Other pathways show stress-dependent modification of GAPDH. Under energy stress, AMPK phosphorylates GAPDH at S122, promoting its interaction with GAP and increasing cell viability. Elevated GAPDH also seems to act through AKT to raise BCL-xL levels, thereby reducing cell death.\n\nMitochondria are another key focus. HK1 and HK2 are shown associated with mitochondria, and their release appears connected to apoptosis. Oxidative stress feeds into a PKM2–BCL2 pathway involving phosphorylation at T69, leading to higher BCL2 and suppression of cell apoptosis.\n\nThe bottom pathway links glucose starvation to lipid metabolism. p38 phosphorylates PFKL at T331, and phosphorylated PFKL interacts with PLIN2 on lipid droplets. This then connects to ATGL and CPT1A, suggesting mobilization of lipids toward mitochondria and ultimately promoting cell survival. Overall, the diagram emphasizes how post-translational modifications of metabolic enzymes help tumor cells adapt to stress and avoid death.",
